\hypertarget{models-and-algorithms-in-the-heartline-pipeline}{%
\subsection{1.2 Models and Algorithms in the HeartLine
Pipeline}\label{models-and-algorithms-in-the-heartline-pipeline}}

This section details the full suite of machine learning models and
algorithms that power the HeartLine diagnostic system, emphasizing their
integration within a privacy-first federated learning framework
\protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}12{]}}}. The chapter is
organized into three major sections: Federated AI infrastructure, deep
learning architectures for ECG analysis, and deployment/optimization
strategies.

\hypertarget{federated-ai-infrastructure-and-security}{%
\subsubsection{1.2.1 Federated AI Infrastructure and
Security}\label{federated-ai-infrastructure-and-security}}

\textbf{a) Federated Averaging and Client-Server Coordination}

HeartLine utilizes the Federated Averaging (FedAvg) algorithm to perform
distributed training across hospitals, clinics, and mobile health units.
Each participating device locally trains a model on its data, sending
only weight updates to the central server. The server aggregates these
updates and broadcasts the improved model back to all clients.

This approach minimizes raw data transfer and supports model
generalization across diverse Algerian populations. A round-based
training cycle allows for continuous learning while ensuring privacy
compliance with local and international regulations.

\textbf{b) Homomorphic Encryption for Secure Aggregation}

To prevent unauthorized data access, HeartLine integrates Homomorphic
Encryption (HE). This cryptographic method enables arithmetic operations
on encrypted model updates, ensuring the central server never sees raw
values. Even in the event of a breach, patient confidentiality is
preserved, strengthening trust among stakeholders.

\textbf{c) Differential Privacy for Update Anonymization}

To further obscure individual patient data contributions, HeartLine
applies Differential Privacy (DP) by injecting controlled Gaussian noise
into each client\textquotesingle s model updates. This provides a
mathematically provable privacy guarantee while maintaining model
performance.

\textbf{d) Communication Protocol and Pseudocode}

HeartLine's server executes the following pseudocode per communication
round:

1. Initialize global weights w\_0.

2. For each round t:

a. Send w\_t to clients.

b. Clients train locally to obtain w\textquotesingle\_k,t+1.

c. Compute update: Delta w\_k = w\textquotesingle\_k - w\_t.

d. Clip: Delta w\_k = Delta w\_k / max(1, \textbar\textbar Delta
w\_k\textbar\textbar{} / C).

e. Add noise: Delta w\_k\_DP = Delta w\_k + N(0, sigma\^{}2).

f. Send Delta w\_k\_DP (HE encrypted) to server.

g. Aggregate: Delta W = average(Delta w\_k\_DP).

h. Update global: w\_t+1 = w\_t + Delta W.

This secure aggregation protocol enables compliant, scalable learning
across distributed Algerian healthcare networks.

\hypertarget{deep-learning-architectures-for-ecg-analysis}{%
\subsubsection{1.2.2 Deep Learning Architectures for ECG
Analysis}\label{deep-learning-architectures-for-ecg-analysis}}

\textbf{a) 1D Convolutional Neural Networks (1D-CNNs)}

1D-CNNs are used at the edge for efficient ECG signal analysis. These
models identify P-waves, QRS complexes, and T-wave anomalies.
Optimizations such as quantization and pruning make them deployable on
resource-limited devices
\protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}13{]}}}.

The below table concisely explains the practical techniques HeartLine
employs to make powerful AI models suitable for edge computing. These
optimizations are crucial for achieving rapid, on-site preliminary
diagnoses, especially vital for immediate triage in remote settings
where computational resources are scarce.

\textbf{Table 08: 1D-CNN Optimizations for Edge Device Deployment in
HeartLine}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.1769}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3232}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.4998}}@{}}
\toprule()
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Optimization Technique}
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Description}
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Benefits for Edge Devices / Rural Compatibility}
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule()
\endhead
\textbf{Model Quantization} & Reduces weight/activation precision (e.g.,
32-bit to 8-bit integers). & Significantly smaller model size, faster
inference, lower power consumption. Critical for unreliable power and
limited bandwidth. \\
\textbf{Pruning} & Removes redundant or less important network
connections (weights). & Reduces model size and computational
complexity, lowers hardware cost. \\
\textbf{Efficient Architectures} & Uses lightweight CNN designs (e.g.,
MobileNet variations for 1D). & Optimizes for minimal parameters and
computations, boosting speed. \\
\textbf{Hardware Acceleration} & Leverages specialized hardware (mobile
GPUs, AI accelerators). & Enables much faster on-device inference for
real-time diagnosis. \\
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\textbf{Source :} Han et al. (2015), outlining quantization, pruning,
and deployment strategies for CNNs on resource-limited devices---ideal
for HeartLine's edge compatibility.

\textbf{Figure 08: Typical 1D-CNN Architecture for ECG Analysis}

\includegraphics[width=5.11806in,height=3.4873in]{vertopal_dd30f619b6ef4e779a1ab8ccfd789402/media/image10.png}

\textbf{Source :} Yuniarti, A.\,R., Rizal, S., \& Lim, K.\,M. (2024).
