This table details a rigorous, human-in-the-loop validation process for
synthetic ECGs generated by GANs. This crucial step ensures that the
augmented data is not just quantitatively accurate but also
qualitatively realistic and clinically valuable, building trust in the
models trained with this synthetic data.

\textbf{e) Model Ensemble and Accuracy Tradeoffs}

HeartLine occasionally combines CNNs, GNNs, and expert features in
ensemble models to balance recall, precision, and computational
efficiency. These hybrid models improve diagnostic robustness,
especially in multi-label ECG classification tasks.

\hypertarget{deployment-interpretability-and-optimization-strategies}{%
\subsubsection{1.2.3 Deployment, Interpretability, and Optimization
Strategies}\label{deployment-interpretability-and-optimization-strategies}}

\textbf{a) Lightweight Edge Deployments (Quantization, Pruning, etc.)}

HeartLine deploys 1D-CNNs on Raspberry Pi and mobile GPUs. Through
quantization (reducing 32-bit weights to 8-bit) and pruning, inference
becomes feasible even in bandwidth- and power-constrained settings
\protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}17{]}}}.

\textbf{b) Knowledge Distillation for Model Compression}

A large, accurate "teacher" model is used to train a smaller "student"
model that mimics its output. This significantly reduces the model size
while retaining high performance, making it ideal for real-time
applications in rural clinics.

\textbf{c) Expert Feature Extraction and Hybrid Modeling}

HeartLine integrates handcrafted ECG features---like HRV metrics, QRS
width, ST segment deviations---into its pipeline. These features feed
traditional classifiers or serve as regularization signals for deep
networks.

The subsequent table provides a detailed breakdown of the
physiologically meaningful features extracted by
HeartLine\textquotesingle s pipeline. This expert-driven feature
engineering complements the deep learning models by providing robust,
interpretable inputs, particularly valuable for scenarios with limited
data or for ensuring clinical explainability
\protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}18{]}}}.

\textbf{Table 10: Categories of Expert Features Extracted from ECG
Signals}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.1836}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.5645}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2519}}@{}}
