in Biology and Medicine}, 133, 104394.

The expert feature extraction pipeline is shown in \textbf{Figure A.11
(Appendix A)}.

This diagram illustrates how HeartLine extracts clinically relevant
features from ECG data, enhancing diagnostic accuracy for complex
arrhythmias. It supports the platform's technical performance, as
validated in section 1.3.2, and aligns with clinical trust requirements
in MENA's healthcare market (section 3.1.3).

\textbf{d) Feature Reduction with PCA and t-SNE}

Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbor
Embedding (t-SNE) are employed to reduce high-dimensional expert
features, boosting interpretability and reducing overfitting risk.

\textbf{e) Explainability and Clinical Interpretability}

HeartLine employs XAI tools like SHAP, LIME, and attention heatmaps.
Combined with human-in-the-loop validation, this ensures trust,
especially when diagnosing edge cases like silent infarctions or
arrhythmic tachycardias.

\hypertarget{deep-convolutional-models-cnn-and-resnet}{%
\subsubsection{1.2.4 Deep Convolutional Models: CNN and
ResNet}\label{deep-convolutional-models-cnn-and-resnet}}

Deep learning methods, especially Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)
and Residual Networks (ResNets), form the computational backbone of
HeartLine\textquotesingle s ECG diagnostic capability. These models are
specifically designed to capture local spatial hierarchies and temporal
dependencies within time-series data like ECGs.

\textbf{a) Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs)}

CNNs are especially adept at detecting recurring signal motifs such as
QRS complexes, T-wave anomalies, or ST-segment deviations across raw ECG
signals \protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}19{]}}}.

