\textbf{b) Key Curation Challenges}

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Label Inconsistency}: Standardizing multi-source annotations.
\item
  \textbf{Noise Handling}: Artifact removal via bandpass filtering and
  wavelet transforms.
\item
  \textbf{Class Imbalance}: Resolved using oversampling, augmentation,
  and GAN synthesis.
\item
  \textbf{Lead Configuration Normalization}: Aligning datasets with
  12-lead conventions.
\item
  \textbf{Ethical Compliance}: All local data collected under
  IRB-approved protocols, complying with Algerian Decree No. 22-187
  (2022) \protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}21{]}}}.
\end{itemize}

\hypertarget{technical-benchmarking-and-validation}{%
\subsubsection{1.3.2 Technical Benchmarking and
Validation}\label{technical-benchmarking-and-validation}}

\textbf{a) Centralized vs. Federated Performance}

Benchmarking validates HeartLine\textquotesingle s algorithms against
established baselines and state-of-the-art methods, confirming the
feasibility and effectiveness of its core components (Federated
Learning, GANs, and classification models).

The preprocessing workflow for ECG signals is illustrated in
\textbf{Figure A.10 (Appendix A)}.

This diagram outlines critical steps, such as noise reduction and QRS
detection, to prepare ECG data for AI analysis, ensuring model
robustness. It supports the high accuracy reported in section 1.3.2,
enabling reliable diagnostics in diverse clinical settings, including
rural Algeria.

The federated learning privacy and security framework is detailed in
\textbf{Figure A.6 (Appendix A)}.

This diagram outlines HeartLine's privacy-preserving mechanisms,
including differential privacy, secure aggregation, and homomorphic
encryption, ensuring data remains local. It reinforces the marginal
performance delta (1-2\%) validated in section 1.3.2, a key
differentiator over centralized competitors like AliveCor (section 3.2).

The next table demonstrates a crucial insight: the performance drop when
moving from centralized to federated learning is \textbf{marginal
(typically 1-2\%)}. This minor trade-off is negligible compared to the
significant privacy benefits and access to larger, more diverse datasets
distributed across multiple institutions, which would otherwise be
impossible to centralize due to privacy concerns. This data underpins
the technical feasibility and strategic rationale for HeartLine's
federated approach \protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}22{]}}}.

