\hypertarget{statistical-features-per-lead-8-featureslead}{%
\subsubsection{2.2.1 Statistical Features per Lead (8
features/lead)}\label{statistical-features-per-lead-8-featureslead}}

Signal quality is assessed using a pipeline of automated quality control
procedures:

\textbf{Table 30: ECG Signal Quality Metrics and Thresholds}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2050}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3334}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.4616}}@{}}
\toprule()
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Metric}
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Description}
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Threshold/Action}
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule()
\endhead
\textbf{SNR} & Signal-to-noise ratio calculated across diagnostic bands
& SNR \textgreater{} 10 dB \\
\textbf{Baseline Wander} & Measured via polynomial detrending &
\textless0.5 mV drift \\
\textbf{Powerline Interference} & Detected using spectral peaks &
Adaptive notch filter (50/60Hz) \\
\textbf{Artifact Detection} & Identifies motion/electrode noise &
Wavelet-based classifier \\
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\textbf{Source}: Adapted from Satija et al. (2017), Clifford et al.
(2012).

\hypertarget{frequency-domain-fourier-features-25lead}{%
\subsubsection{2.2.2 Frequency Domain \& Fourier Features
(25/lead)}\label{frequency-domain-fourier-features-25lead}}

The preprocessing stages apply a sequence of targeted filters:
\protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}28{]}}}

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{Baseline Wander Removal}: Savitzky-Golay smoothing + high-pass
  Butterworth (0.5 Hz)
\item
  \textbf{Powerline Suppression}: IIR notch filter (Q=30) with
  auto-frequency detection
\item
  \textbf{Muscle Noise Reduction}: Discrete Wavelet Transform
  (Daubechies-10, 6-level)
\item
  \textbf{Normalization}: Z-score (μ=0, σ=1), ±5σ outlier capping
\end{itemize}

These preprocessing stages form the foundation of high-integrity ECG
analysis. Each filtering layer is designed to target specific noise
artifacts while minimizing disruption to diagnostically relevant
features. By addressing baseline drift, electronic interference, and
motion-induced noise in succession, the pipeline ensures a clean and
standardized input signal.

The normalization step further harmonizes amplitude scales across
recordings, which is essential for model consistency. This careful
orchestration of enhancement steps supports downstream feature
extraction, both handcrafted and deep learning-based. Ultimately, it
ensures that clinical signals are preserved, not distorted, thereby
increasing the system's trustworthiness in real-world medical settings.
The result is a robust and transparent signal processing core, ready for
scalable deployment.

\hypertarget{information-theoretic-measures-e.g.-entropy}{%
\subsubsection{2.2.3 Information-Theoretic Measures (e.g.,
entropy)}\label{information-theoretic-measures-e.g.-entropy}}

