\hypertarget{feature-fusion-strategies-resnet-34-and-cnn}{%
\subsubsection{2.2.5 Feature Fusion Strategies (ResNet-34 and
CNN)}\label{feature-fusion-strategies-resnet-34-and-cnn}}

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{Concatenation}: 468 (expert) + 1024 (ResNet) = 1,492-dim
  vector
\item
  \textbf{Attention-based}: Dynamic fusion weights based on input
  morphology
\item
  \textbf{Hierarchical}: Clinical features influence early layers; deep
  features refine later stages
\end{itemize}

The following figure illustrates a hybrid architecture combining
expert-engineered ECG features with deep learning outputs from ResNet.
Both streams are processed in parallel and fused using methods like
concatenation or attention. The design balances clinical
interpretability with machine-learned precision. It highlights how
integrating human knowledge and AI enhances diagnostic accuracy
\protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}31{]}}}.

\textbf{Figure 11: Hybrid Feature Fusion Architecture}

\includegraphics[width=7.35096in,height=5.95833in]{vertopal_dd30f619b6ef4e779a1ab8ccfd789402/media/image13.png}

\textbf{Source :} Established by researchers based on standard
multimodal fusion strategies in ECG analysis.

\hypertarget{evaluation-of-dl-fl-and-gnn-models}{%
\subsection{2.3 Evaluation of DL, FL, and GNN
Models}\label{evaluation-of-dl-fl-and-gnn-models}}

\hypertarget{evaluation-metrics-accuracy-auc-roc-f1}{%
\subsubsection{2.3.1 Evaluation Metrics (Accuracy, AUC, ROC,
F1)}\label{evaluation-metrics-accuracy-auc-roc-f1}}

The multi-label classification problem inherent in ECG diagnosis
necessitates the use of robust and clinically interpretable evaluation
metrics. For fair and informative assessment across deep learning (DL),
Federated Learning (FL), and Graph Neural Network (GNN) models, we
employ the following:

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{Accuracy}: Useful for balanced datasets but less informative
  under class imbalance.
\item
  \textbf{F1 Score (Micro \& Macro)}: Balances precision and recall;
  micro-F1 prioritizes prevalent classes, while macro-F1 gives equal
  weight to rare classes.
\item
  \textbf{AUC-ROC}: Measures discrimination capability across all
  classification thresholds; preferred for multi-label tasks.
\item
  \textbf{AUC-PR}: More informative in imbalanced datasets where rare
  class detection is critical.
\item
  \textbf{Hamming Loss}: Quantifies the frequency of incorrect label
  assignments.
\item
  \textbf{Subset Accuracy}: Demands complete match across all labels per
  sample.
\item
  \textbf{Label Ranking Loss}: Evaluates the quality of label ordering,
  crucial in prioritization-based diagnostics.
\end{itemize}

Performance is measured using standard multi-label metrics to capture
clinical significance and statistical robustness:
\protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}32{]}}}

\textbf{Table 34: Overview of Multi-Label Evaluation Metrics}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3506}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2904}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3590}}@{}}
\toprule()
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Metric}
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Use Case}
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\textbf{Notes}
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule()
\endhead
\textbf{F1 Score (Micro/Macro)} & Performance balance & Weighted by
class size \\
\textbf{AUC-ROC} & Discrimination & Best for binary/multi-label \\
\textbf{AUC-PR} & Rare class detection & Imbalance-aware \\
\textbf{Hamming Loss} & Label-wise error & Lower = better \\
\textbf{Subset Accuracy} & Exact match rate & Strict metric \\
\textbf{Label Ranking Loss} & Order quality &
Prioritization-sensitive \\
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\textbf{Source}: Derived from Sokolova \& Lapalme (2009).

