\hypertarget{cnn-and-resnet-classification-benchmarks}{%
\subsubsection{2.3.2 CNN and ResNet Classification
Benchmarks}\label{cnn-and-resnet-classification-benchmarks}}

Baseline DL models were developed using 1D-CNN and ResNet architectures
trained on ECG segments. Key characteristics:

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{Input}: Normalized 10-second ECG segments from datasets
  including MIT-BIH and PhysioNet Challenge.
\item
  \textbf{CNN Baseline}: Shallow architecture with three convolutional
  layers and dropout regularization.
\item
  \textbf{ResNet Implementation}: Deep architecture enabling feature
  reuse and learning of complex cardiac waveform patterns.
\end{itemize}

CNN models achieved 84\% macro-F1, with higher recall for common
arrhythmias. ResNet models improved rare class detection by
\textasciitilde7\% but required more compute resources.

\hypertarget{federated-learning-results-on-mnistcifar-10}{%
\subsubsection{2.3.3 Federated Learning: Results on
MNIST/CIFAR-10}\label{federated-learning-results-on-mnistcifar-10}}

To validate FL strategies before applying them to ECG, we benchmarked on
standard datasets:

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{MNIST (Digit Classification)}:

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    Centralized F1: 97.5\% \textbar{} FL-FedAvg: 96.8\%
    \protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}33{]}}}
  \item
    Delta \textless{} 1\%, validating model convergence with data
    privacy.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{CIFAR-10 (Image Recognition)}:

  \begin{itemize}
  \tightlist
  \item
    Centralized Top-1 Accuracy: 85.2\% \textbar{} FL Accuracy: 83.7\%
    \protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}34{]}}}
  \item
    Demonstrated feasibility of federated GNN/CNN deployment under
    non-IID partitioning.
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

The experimental settings were aligned with sinjoysaha's Kaggle FL
notebook. These results support federated deployment in ECG scenarios,
with expected minimal degradation and privacy preservation.

\hypertarget{graph-neural-network-ecg-modeling}{%
\subsubsection{2.3.4 Graph Neural Network ECG
Modeling}\label{graph-neural-network-ecg-modeling}}

GNNs allow modeling of spatial and temporal relationships across 12-lead
ECGs, treating each lead as a node within a physiological graph.

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  \textbf{Graph Construction}: Nodes = ECG leads; Edges =
  functional/positional relationships (e.g., limb vs precordial).
\item
  \textbf{Message Passing}: Information propagation across leads mimics
  spatiotemporal cardiac dynamics.
\item
  \textbf{Federated GNN Training}: Local updates are aggregated at a
  central server using FedAvg or adaptive federated optimizers
  (FedProx).
\end{itemize}

Experiments on PhysioNet and LUDB datasets revealed:
\protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}35{]}}}

\begin{itemize}
\tightlist
\item
  GNN + FL models achieved 88\% macro-F1, outperforming ResNet in rare
  arrhythmia detection.
\item
  Class activation mappings showed interpretable attention to clinically
  relevant leads.
\end{itemize}

