\hypertarget{system-infrastructure-and-deployment}{%
\subsection{3.2 System Infrastructure and
Deployment}\label{system-infrastructure-and-deployment}}

The realization of HeartLine\textquotesingle s vision requires a robust,
secure, and scalable technical infrastructure. This section details the
production pipeline, system architecture, and technical specifications
designed to support the system\textquotesingle s deployment across
diverse healthcare settings in Algeria.

\hypertarget{technical-pipeline-and-hardware-design}{%
\subsubsection{3.2.1 Technical Pipeline and Hardware
Design}\label{technical-pipeline-and-hardware-design}}

The HeartLine production pipeline encompasses the complete lifecycle of
ECG data, from its raw acquisition to the final diagnostic output and
continuous model improvement. This pipeline is designed for efficiency,
reliability, and security.

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\alph{enumi})}
\item
  \textbf{Data Ingestion and Edge Preprocessing:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Acquisition:} Raw ECG signals are acquired from
    patient-facing ECG devices (e.g., standard 12-lead machines,
    portable single-lead devices) at local clinics or even through
    potential future patient-worn wearables.
  \item
    \textbf{Secure Transfer to Edge:} The raw ECG data is securely
    transferred (e.g., via local Wi-Fi, Bluetooth) to the HeartLine edge
    device (e.g., Raspberry Pi 4).
  \item
    \textbf{Edge Preprocessing:} The lightweight edge device performs
    immediate, on-device preprocessing to clean the raw signal.

    \begin{itemize}
    \item
      \begin{quote}
      \textbf{Pseudocode for Edge Preprocessing:}\\
      \strut \\
      def preprocess\_ecg\_edge(raw\_ecg\_signal, sampling\_rate):\\
      \# 1. Baseline Wander Removal (e.g., high-pass filter)\\
      \# Apply a Butterworth high-pass filter (0.5 Hz cutoff)\\
      nyquist = 0.5 * sampling\_rate\\
      cutoff\_freq = 0.5 / nyquist\\
      b, a = signal.butter(order=3, Wn=cutoff\_freq,
      btype=\textquotesingle high\textquotesingle, analog=False)\\
      filtered\_ecg = signal.filtfilt(b, a, raw\_ecg\_signal)\\
      \# 2. Power Line Interference Removal (e.g., Notch filter for
      50/60 Hz)\\
      \# Assuming 50 Hz power line noise for Algeria\\
      b\_notch, a\_notch = signal.iirnotch(50.0, Q=30,
      fs=sampling\_rate)\\
      denoised\_ecg = signal.filtfilt(b\_notch, a\_notch,
      filtered\_ecg)\\
      \# 3. QRS Detection (e.g., Pan-Tompkins algorithm, simplified
      version)\\
      \# (Actual Pan-Tompkins is complex, this is a placeholder for
      detection)\\
      qrs\_peaks = find\_qrs\_peaks\_simplified(denoised\_ecg) \#
      Placeholder function\\
      \# 4. Segmentation (e.g., fixed-length windows around QRS or whole
      signal)\\
      \# For simplicity, let\textquotesingle s assume segmenting into
      10-second clips\\
      segment\_length = 10 * sampling\_rate \# 10 seconds of samples\\
      segmented\_ecg = {[}denoised\_ecg{[}i:i + segment\_length{]}\\
      for i in range(0, len(denoised\_ecg), segment\_length)\\
      if len(denoised\_ecg{[}i:i + segment\_length{]}) ==
      segment\_length{]}\\
      \# 5. Normalization (e.g., Min-Max or Z-score for each segment)\\
      normalized\_segments = {[}{]}\\
      for segment in segmented\_ecg:\\
      mean = np.mean(segment)\\
      std = np.std(segment)\\
      normalized\_segments.append((segment - mean) / (std + 1e-8)) \#
      Add epsilon for stability\\
      return normalized\_segments, qrs\_peaks \# Return segments ready
      for inference/features
      \end{quote}
    \end{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Anonymization:} Patient identifiers are immediately stripped
    or tokenized, ensuring only anonymized ECGs proceed.
  \item
    \textbf{Local Storage:} The anonymized, preprocessed ECGs are
    securely stored locally on the edge device or a local server within
    the clinic, adhering to data residency requirements.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Local Inference (Edge AI):
  \protect\hyperlink{references}{{[}37{]}}}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    The lightweight 1D-CNN model (optimized for edge) performs rapid
    preliminary diagnosis on the preprocessed ECG segments.
  \item
    This provides immediate feedback to the local clinician, enabling
    faster triage.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Secure Data and Model Update Transmission:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    Anonymized ECG data (if consented for secondary use like quality
    control), along with locally trained model updates
    (gradients/weights), are prepared for transmission.
  \item
    \textbf{Privacy-Preserving Layer:} Differential Privacy noise is
    added, and homomorphic encryption is applied to the model updates.
  \item
    \textbf{Secure Communication:} Encrypted data and model updates are
    transmitted over a secure, authenticated channel (e.g., mTLS-secured
    HTTPS) to the central Federated Learning server.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Centralized Processing and Global Model Management:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    \textbf{Data Aggregation (Metadata/Anonymized Data):} The central
    server aggregates anonymized metadata (e.g., diagnostic codes,
    demographic counts) and potentially some consented, fully anonymized
    ECG snippets for quality assurance or auditing, but \emph{never raw
    patient data}.
  \item
    \textbf{Federated Model Aggregation:} The encrypted, differentially
    private model updates from client nodes are aggregated using FedAvg
    to update the global AI model. This occurs in a secure enclave
    (e.g., using Intel SGX or similar TEEs for added security).
  \item
    \textbf{Global Model Storage \& Versioning:} The updated global
    model is stored and versioned in a secure cloud environment.
  \item
    \textbf{Centralized Inference (Optional/Complex Cases):} For complex
    or ambiguous cases flagged by edge models, the full 12-lead ECG
    (anonymized and securely transmitted if consented) can be processed
    by the more powerful central ResNet/GNN model for a definitive
    diagnosis \protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}38{]}}}.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Diagnostic Reporting and Clinician Interface:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    Diagnostic results (from either edge or central inference) are
    formatted into standardized reports (HL7/FHIR compatible).
  \item
    These reports, along with the original ECG visualizations, are
    presented to clinicians via a secure web-based or mobile dashboard.
  \item
    \textbf{Clinician Feedback Loop:} Clinicians can provide feedback on
    AI diagnoses, which is critical for continuous model improvement and
    re-annotation efforts.
  \end{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Continuous Learning and Model Improvement:}

  \begin{itemize}
  \item
    The federated learning cycle continuously updates the global model
    as new data is processed across the network of clinics.
  \item
    Periodically, a new, improved global model version is deployed back
    to the edge devices and central inference engines.
  \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

The application workflow for HeartLine's diagnostic platform is detailed
in \textbf{Figure A.1 (Appendix A)}.

This diagram illustrates the end-to-end process of ECG data processing
and diagnosis, showcasing the seamless integration of edge and central
models. It highlights HeartLine's efficiency in delivering real-time
diagnostics, critical for rural Algerian clinics, aligning with the
region's digital health growth (section 3.1.3).

\hypertarget{modular-deployment-stack-edge-cloud}{%
\subsubsection{3.2.2 Modular Deployment Stack (Edge +
Cloud)}\label{modular-deployment-stack-edge-cloud}}

HeartLine\textquotesingle s system architecture is designed to be
modular, distributed, and highly resilient, comprising distinct layers
that interact seamlessly.

HeartLine\textquotesingle s modular architecture is composed of
distributed edge, node, and cloud components:

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \textbf{Edge Layer}: Raspberry Pi 4 devices with TensorFlow Lite
  handle preprocessing and local inference.
\item
  \textbf{Node Layer}: Local clinics store data securely (SQLite) and
  communicate asynchronously with central systems
  \protect\hyperlink{references}{\textbf{{[}39{]}}}.
\item
  \textbf{Cloud Layer}: A central FL server aggregates updates and
  manages global models in a secure, scalable environment (e.g.,
  AWS/GCP/Azure).
\item
  \textbf{Containers and Orchestration}: Dockerized services are
  deployed using Kubernetes for automated scaling, self-healing, and
  efficient resource use.
\item
  \textbf{Data Storage}: MinIO or S3-compatible object storage stores
  model versions, audit logs, and statistical summaries.
\end{itemize}

The system architecture and workflow for HeartLine are presented in
\textbf{Figure A.2 (Appendix A)}.

This diagram combines a high-level view of HeartLine's technical
infrastructure with its operational workflow, emphasizing scalability
and interoperability. It underscores the platform's ability to support
decentralized healthcare settings, a key advantage over competitors like
Philips (section 3.2), and aligns with MENA's digital health trends
(section 3.1.3).

HeartLine's deployment and infrastructure are depicted in \textbf{Figure
A.3 (Appendix A)}.

This diagram details the infrastructure supporting HeartLine's federated
learning and edge deployment, ensuring robust performance across
distributed clinics. Its design supports scalability and
cost-effectiveness, positioning HeartLine favorably in low-resource
settings like rural Algeria (section 3.1.3).

The database architecture for tenant isolation and central aggregation
is shown in \textbf{Figure A.4 (Appendix A)}.

This diagram illustrates how HeartLine ensures data privacy through
tenant isolation while enabling secure model aggregation. It reinforces
compliance with privacy regulations, a critical differentiator in
Algeria's healthcare market (section 3.2), as validated by the federated
learning framework (section 1.3.2).

This table provides an overview of the key tech-components and
specifications of HeartLine.

