\textbf{Financing Strategy:} A Prudent Blend for Initial Launch:

Our initial capital structure reflects a strategic balance: 40\% equity
(12,800,000 DZD) contributed by the founders, demonstrating strong
commitment, and 60\% through a 5-year loan (19,200,000 DZD) at a
competitive 10\% interest rate. This blend ensures sufficient initial
funding while distributing financial risk.

\textbf{Loan Repayment:} The loan has a clear repayment schedule of
\textbf{5,040,000 DZD per year}, a fixed obligation we have incorporated
into our long-term financial planning.

\hypertarget{revenue-model-and-unit-economics}{%
\subsubsection{4.3.2 Revenue Model and Unit
Economics}\label{revenue-model-and-unit-economics}}

HeartLine generates income via B2B SaaS subscriptions and diagnostic
usage fees. Its subscription model supports monthly recurring revenue,
supplemented by per-ECG charges in hospital settings.

\textbf{Turnover:} Projecting Market Adoption and Revenue Growth:

Our revenue forecasts are presented in two scenarios, optimistic and
pessimistic, providing a realistic range of potential outcomes for Year
1.

The succeeding scenario models a high-growth trajectory based on strong
pilot outcomes, rapid onboarding capacity, and low early-stage
attrition. Under these conditions, HeartLine expands its active clinic
base from 0 to 111 across 12 months, onboarding 121 clinics with minimal
churn. Revenue scales with average monthly clinic activity, culminating
in 40.6 million DZD by year-end. This projection assumes high adoption
rates in underserved areas, bolstered by our compliance readiness,
attractive pricing, and early clinical endorsements.

